<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / BenQ ScreenBar flickering</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Lighting troubleshooting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">BenQ ScreenBar flickering: identify the model and power path first.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">“ScreenBar” covers several products with different power requirements and controls. A troubleshooting step that applies to the original ScreenBar can be wrong for ScreenBar Halo or ScreenBar Pro, so start with the label on the lamp or its power specification.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Identify the exact model, use the supplied cable/adapter or a power source that meets that model’s BenQ specification, and test whether the flicker is visible to your eyes or only on camera. If the lamp still visibly flickers on a supported power source, test the supplied controls/cable and contact BenQ rather than improvising repairs.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Model check</p><h2>The three common models do not have identical electrical requirements.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>ScreenBar</strong><p>BenQ lists 5V DC, maximum 1A, 5W.</p></div>
<div><strong>ScreenBar Halo</strong><p>BenQ lists 5V DC, maximum 1.3A. The Halo uses a wireless controller with its own batteries.</p></div>
<div><strong>ScreenBar Pro</strong><p>BenQ lists 5V DC, maximum 1.7A / 8.5W and uses a different control design from Halo.</p></div>
</div>
<p>Use the current specification page for the exact revision you own. Do not assume a monitor USB port that powers one model is adequate for another.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Test the documented power path.</h2>
<p>Use BenQ’s supplied cable and, where the product includes one, the supplied power adapter. If you have been powering the lamp from a monitor USB port, compare that port’s documented output with the ScreenBar model’s requirement. A connector that fits does not establish that the source can provide the required current.</p>
<p>Change only the power source during the test. If the flicker disappears on the supported source, the previous power path was the problem.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Decide whether the lamp flickers in person or only in recorded video.</h2>
<p>If the light looks steady to your eyes but bands or flickers in a webcam or phone recording, do not immediately treat that as an LED-driver failure. Camera shutter/exposure timing can interact with the way a light is modulated. Try the camera’s anti-flicker setting, exposure, or shutter options and compare at a different brightness level.</p>
<p>If you can see the lamp itself flicker with your eyes, continue troubleshooting the lamp and power path.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Keep model-specific controls separate.</h2>
<p>Do not follow Halo controller instructions on a ScreenBar Pro or vice versa. If a controller is unresponsive, use the exact model’s BenQ manual/support procedure. Avoid cleaning internal electrical contacts or opening the lamp unless BenQ documents that procedure for the model.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need a replacement?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If a supported power source or camera setting removes the symptom, keep the light.</p></div>
<div><strong>Maybe</strong><p>If a detachable cable or external power source is part of the model, substitute only a known-compatible part before blaming the lamp.</p></div>
<div><strong>Service / replacement</strong><p>If visible flicker persists with the supplied or documented power path and controls, use BenQ support or warranty service instead of opening the light.</p></div>
</div></section>

